Journal Entry – Week 3
Harm Analysis Framework
Part 1 — Speculative Harm Analysis (Before Readings)
System Chosen:
Automated Hiring Algorithm used by companies to screen job applicants

1.1 What the System Does
The hiring algorithm evaluates résumés and application materials to decide which candidates advance to interviews. It may score applicants based on keywords, past experience, education, or inferred traits.

1.2 Who the Stakeholders Are
Job applicants

Hiring managers and HR staff

The company using the system

Communities affected by employment opportunities

Regulators and labor rights organizations

1.3 Speculative Harms (Before Readings)
Harm 1 — Discrimination and Bias
The algorithm may favor certain demographics (e.g., men, certain ethnic groups, certain universities) if trained on biased historical hiring data.

Harm 2 — Exclusion of Qualified Candidates
Applicants with unconventional backgrounds or non‑standard résumés may be filtered out automatically.

Harm 3 — Lack of Transparency
Applicants may not know why they were rejected or how to improve their chances.

Harm 4 — Reinforcement of Inequality
If the system systematically excludes certain groups, it can worsen existing socioeconomic disparities.

Harm 5 — Psychological and Emotional Harm
Automated rejection without explanation can create feelings of helplessness or unfairness.

Part 2 — Reading‑Informed Updates
After reading the assigned materials on harm, fairness, and sociotechnical systems, I refined my understanding of harms and added new categories.

2.1 New Insights from Readings
Insight 1 — Harms Can Be Downstream and Systemic
Harms are not only immediate; they can accumulate over time and across institutions. A biased hiring algorithm can influence entire career trajectories.

Insight 2 — Harms Can Be Non‑Material
Emotional, dignitary, and representational harms matter even when no material loss occurs.

Insight 3 — Systems Can Create New Harms
Automated systems may introduce harms that did not exist in human‑only hiring, such as algorithmic opacity or data‑driven stereotyping.

Insight 4 — Harms Are Distributed Unevenly
Some groups bear disproportionate risk (e.g., women, immigrants, people with disabilities).

Part 3 — Revised Harm Analysis (After Readings)
3.1 Updated Harm Categories
Harm Category A — Allocative Harms
These harms involve the distribution of opportunities or resources.

Qualified candidates may be denied interviews.

Certain groups may be systematically excluded from employment.

The algorithm may amplify historical inequalities.

Harm Category B — Representational Harms
These harms involve how groups are portrayed or valued.

The system may encode stereotypes (e.g., associating leadership with male‑coded language).

Applicants from marginalized groups may be implicitly treated as “less desirable.”

Harm Category C — Procedural Harms
These harms relate to fairness in process.

Lack of transparency in scoring and rejection.

No meaningful way to appeal or correct errors.

Applicants cannot understand or challenge the system’s logic.

Harm Category D — Quality‑of‑Life Harms
These harms affect well‑being.

Emotional distress from unexplained rejection.

Loss of confidence or trust in hiring systems.

Increased stress for applicants who cannot tailor their résumés to opaque criteria.

Harm Category E — Societal and Structural Harms
These harms affect communities and institutions.

Reinforcement of systemic discrimination.

Reduced workforce diversity.

Long‑term economic impacts on excluded groups.

Part 4 — Reflection on Frameworks
4.1 How the Framework Changed My Thinking
Before the readings, I thought of harm mostly as individual unfairness.
After the readings, I now understand harm as:

multi‑layered (individual, group, societal)

both material and symbolic

often invisible or indirect

embedded in sociotechnical systems

The framework helped me see that harms are not just outcomes but also processes, representations, and structures.

4.2 What the Framework Helped Me Notice
The hiring algorithm does not just reject people; it shapes who gets access to opportunity.

Harms can occur even when the system “works as designed.”

Some harms are hard to measure but still significant.

The system interacts with existing inequalities, often amplifying them.

4.3 Remaining Questions
How can we measure representational harms in algorithmic systems?

What counts as a “fair” hiring process?

Should automated hiring be allowed at all without human oversight?
